  "einstein_unique_among_pre_1905_docs": true,
  "kind": "dcd1_pilot_specificity_check",
  "origin_unique_among_pre_1859_docs": false,
  "purpose": "Test whether the two DCR4 structural signatures (prediction-independence, symmetric equalisation) are UNIQUE to the revolutionary paper on each case, or whether pre-revolutionary papers also hit them.",
  "signature_definitions": {
    "equalisation": "disc(c1) >= 3 AND disc(c2) >= 3 AND min/max >= 0.5",
    "prediction_independence": "n_pred >= 1 AND sum of T1/T2/T3 (or D1/D2/D3) use in predictions == 0"
  },
  "verdict": "MIXED. Einstein 1905 is the UNIQUE document in the DCR arc corpus with both signatures firing meaningfully (T1=4, T2=4, pred_indep=YES). But on the Darwin corpus, THREE pre-Origin documents (Erasmus Darwin 1794, Wallace 1855 Sarawak, Beagle 1845 ch17) also hit both signatures at the same thresholds as Origin chapters. On Darwin, the 'unique to revolutionary paper' claim FAILS. The signatures fire on any paper that has predictions independent of the D-categories AND discusses D1 and D2 at similar counts \u2014 which includes speculative pre-revolutionary work like Zoonomia. This substantially weakens the DCD1 pilot paper's 'replication' claim: the signatures replicate in count, but not in uniqueness."
}
